{"text": "A strong profile begins with what the student can do and what helps the student engage. The profile lists these strengths for Maya: Warm, socially motivated learner; Recognizes familiar environmental print; Enjoys music and food preparation; and Persists with hands-on routines. They give the team meaningful entry points for instruction, motivation, peer connection, and self-advocacy.\n\nHelpful preferences are: Real materials and familiar settings; Short predictable tasks; Immediate meaningful feedback; and Choice between two activities. Respecting these preferences can increase participation and support student choice.\n\nCommunication access must be planned explicitly. Maya’s communication profile includes the following: Uses short spoken phrases plus picture symbols; Answers concrete either/or questions; Uses a break card when overwhelmed; and Needs adults to confirm, not assume, meaning. Staff should communicate directly with Maya, allow the required processing or response time, and confirm meaning without speaking for the student. If communication becomes less effective, check access, fatigue, stress, sensory load, and the communication environment.\n\nThe key principle is to presume competence while making communication reliable. Next, I’ll explain the conditions in which Maya learns best and the barriers that can make learning harder.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-C-03.mp3", "words": [{"text": "A", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.225, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 1}, {"text": "strong", "start": 0.2375, "end": 0.6, "start_char": 2, "end_char": 8}, {"text": "profile", "start": 0.6125, "end": 1.225, "start_char": 9, "end_char": 16}, {"text": "begins", "start": 1.2375, "end": 1.7, "start_char": 17, "end_char": 23}, {"text": "with", "start": 1.7125, "end": 1.8625, "start_char": 24, "end_char": 28}, {"text": "what", "start": 1.875, "end": 2.075, "start_char": 29, "end_char": 33}, {"text": "the", "start": 2.0875, "end": 2.1875, "sta